      "content": "Autoformalization with LLMs Autoformalization is the task of automatically converting informal\ntheorems and proofs into machine-verifiable formats (Wang et al., 2018; Szegedy, 2020). Early\napproaches employed neural machine translation methods to translate texts into the Mizar lan-\nguage (Wang et al., 2020). Recent advancements in LLMs have opened up new possibilities for\nautoformalization. Researchers have explored using few-shot prompting to enable LLMs to translate\nmathematical problems into formal formats, including Isabelle and Lean (Wu et al., 2022; Gadgil\net al., 2022). Other studies ha",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reviews prior work on LLM-based autoformalization and outcome- versus process-supervised verifiers (OSV vs. PSV), contrasting expensive human or Monte Carlo Tree Search process annotation with PDA's use of automatic step-level feedback from the Lean 4 compiler.",
        "state": "draft",
